ent :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247788} {:answer/id #uuid "43c564eb-1cec-5068-bb46-c7d448a4689f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248029} {:answer/id #uuid "60377ceb-0560-5dd4-89d1-60433d96dd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248294} {:answer/id #uuid "b61c8aaa-158b-52aa-930a-c5c6b5128ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249053} {:answer/id #uuid "ff97869d-be3b-5c18-bc98-f3b9c2a66095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61c8aaa-158b-52aa-930a-c5c6b5128ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249053} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f70073-768b-5c21-9eb3-fe3ffbc2f0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d48a1800-0baf-5dcd-a800-d0ee3d7aed53" :question/math-academy-id "q-115435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-1),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} {:answer/id #uuid "6d01c7a7-ed12-581a-857f-30f616b9ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248388} {:answer/id #uuid "711453e3-73f9-5722-820f-ac643ba5fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248413} {:answer/id #uuid "8d999a07-30ce-573b-8c2d-fa8220991104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=4(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248686} {:answer/id #uuid "d6aed572-9d95-5182-8bd1-9ea1faa87eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5ff86e-cbd2-5244-bd7e-fa7501ce1356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247533} :answer-field/id #uuid "242c488e-b1d2-5f9b-924c-0c83035c9cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af2d74a7-36e3-5f6f-95f7-10b5d7e5decf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115451" :question/problem "![](images/97/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e5e768-df1f-5d74-ae7a-7fc38729e30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247488} {:answer/id #uuid "12140e6e-87e7-5362-8ef1-3152c113ebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247586} {:answer/id #uuid "27a91fe5-ec69-5a55-89f5-4cbe9dbddc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247777} {:answer/id #uuid "96f198f9-c262-5f1b-bcbf-33228ef0638e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248771} {:answer/id #uuid "d06f6c37-a466-50b0-b008-678db1636504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12140e6e-87e7-5362-8ef1-3152c113ebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247586} :answer-field/id #uuid "43ab0a42-397a-53a0-87b7-b26848ed22d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af0b97a8-b635-5432-9208-09a4d6d1e1c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115500" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e26f3115e64d22d336c164fca762a1654ce515019f425f5b0b772276ca88f727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 9 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8ad212-90af-5030-91e9-e77eed90aa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{28}(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247853} {:answer/id #uuid "632c0ad0-e7ff-5d95-acad-0af5067a4924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248324} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcf9f6e-2849-59c7-bb97-59ce391b7323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=3(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248528} {:answer/id #uuid "a03b5184-19c0-5cca-be72-38a2008cfc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=3(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248853} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d43b64-b98e-5b9d-8951-94466d0a2d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{28}(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a03b5184-19c0-5cca-be72-38a2008cfc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=3(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248853} :answer-field/id #uuid "27d97fb2-5838-5cec-9d00-faace91d5216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853be803-536b-569b-a3cc-9828143249b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115506" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(2,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+5) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =3(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025ef3a4-70ce-52be-b6d0-841afe01115f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247440} {:answer/id #uuid "a86c5836-b30c-52df-9e07-609b42fb6c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248920} {:answer/id #uuid "c2584ac3-e62f-5e7f-87a1-66f103ed801d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249150} {:answer/id #uuid "d5855c13-f8e9-5c30-922d-3b629e92e2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=5(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249316} {:answer/id #uuid "d72093e1-3f29-5cf1-8fa0-11aa9afee3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86c5836-b30c-52df-9e07-609b42fb6c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6336bb4c-e327-501a-9829-12a627d4800c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92e783e4-7f06-5937-98d3-6bf3624a8108" :question/math-academy-id "q-115509" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,-3).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-(-3)) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (-1-1)^{2} & =4p(2+3) \\\\ 4 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a986a9-dc99-5796-9957-e44b0b8d9b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247416} {:answer/id #uuid "3af46962-42bf-52d0-9044-0ca1c67c1f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247946} {:answer/id #uuid "40f40afb-bd2f-53ad-bfee-4cedf94cb572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248000} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0b7d8e-f5b6-5077-a852-7535675aef4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a6a65-91c8-5dc8-8832-b63911bb283a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a77272b0-ce4e-560d-9231-c7789014a60c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c7e4a7-0bdd-5125-954e-1f3a2bdbf235" :question/math-academy-id "q-115513" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,0)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 9 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142f2f77-a4b7-5b48-bf84-e7dcbd48b69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247605} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddafea7-b413-5ba3-a019-c366151f6773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247831} {:answer/id #uuid "73aab154-b5f4-5037-8868-b6249ce08fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248438} {:answer/id #uuid "79a1dd44-e87e-5396-8592-b303b665b229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248494} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0363c2-ebe9-5343-aca4-ae87280f4870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad0363c2-ebe9-5343-aca4-ae87280f4870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248970} :answer-field/id #uuid "8259a8cd-aeb6-5eb8-84ba-08c5a2aac0b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f96f4a00-e296-51d8-8283-a935ed362b8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115516" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x]^{′}}{{[x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1be917-67fb-578e-954b-abd43a23e348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247974} {:answer/id #uuid "50cbb7df-ff1d-5ec1-b975-423eb45cf6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248148} {:answer/id #uuid "5a30d855-606b-58c6-b70c-741050c6c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248240} {:answer/id #uuid "c1157ca6-9bce-51d1-a9ee-ceec63429c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249140} {:answer/id #uuid "ce75318e-e092-5621-a3d1-bcdfb26a886b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a30d855-606b-58c6-b70c-741050c6c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248240} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8ddb9e-3675-514c-a828-85dac3fe037b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef69b6da-6b79-5e57-a05c-34b900661ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115517" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[x]^{′}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d36f99a-7b3c-58c4-8e9a-3a21fd763566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247544} {:answer/id #uuid "61a4653f-6df7-51f2-86fa-d13ae68764a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248305} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2148f7-50e6-51e7-9398-cf6ddcd9de74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248668} {:answer/id #uuid "a11b1bc5-2153-59a0-816b-97eff84bc81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248863} {:answer/id #uuid "db8f7095-8449-5145-9fef-5d8ca09214f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d36f99a-7b3c-58c4-8e9a-3a21fd763566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247544} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd93bb03-4956-5c3c-8d6a-9abd4a3f4f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4bbbb84-0fac-5f07-a8f5-38985b38226e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115518" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b693811-a16b-53ed-9611-68583bdf136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247672} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4b941c-69b2-577b-a723-cb390aecaad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247825} {:answer/id #uuid "52a8d6c1-c802-52b6-9422-b177b1c39eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248165} {:answer/id #uuid "644a1b11-e2aa-55cd-ba47-d5a074cd81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248329} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0fcf86-6e40-5292-948f-778a8f8317e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644a1b11-e2aa-55cd-ba47-d5a074cd81d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb56519-add1-5a22-b89f-f23b73f9e1db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b7651b-eacd-5715-8331-dab57a1d4302" :question/math-academy-id "q-115526" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "442d1826-8ba8-59fe-af61-e804166eea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248032} {:answer/id #uuid "773d5740-f93f-500c-a1e3-bcf114d1f5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248475} {:answer/id #uuid "83a48ec6-e5bf-5565-8966-159df28981ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248588} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc534d1-7861-539f-acce-b7cca0d2b9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248703} {:answer/id #uuid "e145a029-9b27-5f17-87b3-14bc970c1d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "442d1826-8ba8-59fe-af61-e804166eea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248032} :answer-field/id #uuid "34b86cf6-fd45-5124-ac9e-a3a0687418ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88fb5994-1227-5032-b75a-adafaeaf4ffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-115528" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d037599-07ed-5ba7-83e9-f8c1efa482f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247821} {:answer/id #uuid "31ae4547-e21a-58af-ba30-0bdc4c6faf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247874} {:answer/id #uuid "88431084-d59b-514f-a06a-1a663f1974b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248641} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa8ec-c233-56f4-9f06-28d24d2fad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249296} {:answer/id #uuid "f67649d3-0d90-50b6-8568-82074b23672e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa8ec-c233-56f4-9f06-28d24d2fad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249296} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f9bf5f-e290-5c3d-ab0c-661753693c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e116ffff-24eb-5f64-952b-b5d8dd36f71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115530" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{(\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{8\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6ccd60-a3e4-5ce6-9d5e-a376f267d343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248406} {:answer/id #uuid "71f7119c-74ea-52d7-a308-7659883775e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248418} {:answer/id #uuid "84779d56-58c2-555d-a3cc-90c133ddaec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} {:answer/id #uuid "b961e3af-c091-5872-9218-c014fd5deaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249081} {:answer/id #uuid "fc333b6b-03c6-59c2-97cd-44b7a727e392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84779d56-58c2-555d-a3cc-90c133ddaec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} :answer-field/id #uuid "8264505c-0613-5f02-b2e2-463f0471a6a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bc1f703-1de2-59b6-981e-831238ea97de" :question/math-academy-id "q-115592" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{′}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439853c6-2937-5b91-a8f2-dc8431ada5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248028} {:answer/id #uuid "9034ab44-bfb6-51cc-afa3-631ba91cd9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248705} {:answer/id #uuid "a0de1bab-8f70-530c-869b-42cf67ad5c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248862} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bcb5e0-04c6-5c07-8b21-86f194174368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249317} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d971c4-0509-5ce8-bc45-fbade9865e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "439853c6-2937-5b91-a8f2-dc8431ada5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248028} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8e013a-4dd0-52ac-bae6-4d64d494b145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e94ede6e-b65e-547c-9e4b-813d5e9b97e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115601" :question/problem "![](images/49/4938d034c766b78e1da1a5bd19f2a12b314f772ca4819cd52d4f1463ff1ae3a4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(3,2),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 4 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e36eb8-21f0-5f1c-a082-d66656a6790b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247510} {:answer/id #uuid "289823bd-24bc-52a9-a1b5-d43e40baba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{12}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247785} {:answer/id #uuid "852a3f40-3dd3-50b3-949a-26583a2c107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248609} {:answer/id #uuid "9a587bd0-68cc-56a2-bdb6-582856b6ff94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248799} {:answer/id #uuid "e730056c-bdc5-54d5-a69b-d38cfd63db08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a587bd0-68cc-56a2-bdb6-582856b6ff94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248799} :answer-field/id #uuid "7665e01f-6bd4-5975-9b08-e87552004f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b404c30-267f-56f0-958b-85883e368d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-115602" :question/problem "![](images/57/5746a438eccdd55d345f1c71a768f971aa1a342798743e151f7557102ddd8783.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(1,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(1,-3),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-3)^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a8cc45c-a34a-53f6-8aef-19e7dde9ab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248087} {:answer/id #uuid "79364bd6-0177-5c87-baf3-c1208d7a4d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248488} {:answer/id #uuid "8ada4a96-61b9-5028-b037-83a44b2109fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248662} {:answer/id #uuid "e886bd4d-b191-56ad-9f08-468f250218a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249479} {:answer/id #uuid "eea18b97-4f5c-594c-82da-685342d0ffe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79364bd6-0177-5c87-baf3-c1208d7a4d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248488} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd6cddd-26c0-5b1e-8ffc-6809c2807173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8aa75b6-dda5-58c9-ab6d-95e4d74e59b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115603" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b472d30e6260b0e0f1ce4bc791c5ce3068d78a304711775e1327a4c3dbd8c3d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(2,-2),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043d1ce5-9e3e-5519-9a4c-03569c147664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247455} {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} {:answer/id #uuid "64b65e65-4617-5e48-b1cf-a0372e6395b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{3}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248333} {:answer/id #uuid "7379ce95-99ed-5b20-9302-b338646b620c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248436} {:answer/id #uuid "d091e770-03db-512b-a3b7-40a0f2053ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aca0b8a-ad9d-5b96-a5a6-ae937ea506b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "513d3507-74c5-5ea9-ac09-4c99e11dedbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115623" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a5a701-4a51-5b68-8e1b-b66e6b850789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247601} {:answer/id #uuid "1f586b44-1122-5f1d-b05e-8923133fd7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247701} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec8b296-2455-5f5c-91c3-439121becad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247843} {:answer/id #uuid "3add8fbd-a7ff-5f7b-a16d-de66af3fe046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247945} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb054cf-6d87-53c2-b259-43caf88b5ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a5a701-4a51-5b68-8e1b-b66e6b850789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247601} :answer-field/id #uuid "c569f12e-cea8-5587-81d8-e362cff29dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69e800ff-6717-5a96-b9a0-b74b69055c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115625" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b14a6063e0b1be751e738060087644cacaa3c3cf1e324ee66bf57232834e618d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,1),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {1}^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5070b1-5b1a-5296-a031-e24091f2b3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247940} {:answer/id #uuid "59abf281-9efe-56d8-8676-d28dc59a654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248235} {:answer/id #uuid "716b26ff-dd27-556a-9144-0afa8fa39742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{8}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248414} {:answer/id #uuid "9872c090-a386-58d7-88fa-9f558a1e3b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248784} {:answer/id #uuid "efe6cb33-161c-5882-8edc-73409d4ba88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59abf281-9efe-56d8-8676-d28dc59a654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248235} :answer-field/id #uuid "884a7b9a-4071-5cf6-a767-f08c0dbe1f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77a2fd6b-e9d0-50f6-a357-c099577e8b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-115628" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(6,3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {3}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 9 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{8})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5d417c-3f72-5468-9639-1f5120b9f091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247522} {:answer/id #uuid "21a8fb1b-59d9-55a8-aee3-2f69f0e83e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247723} {:answer/id #uuid "31a33578-5df7-576f-8acf-c92eae34ca82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247873} {:answer/id #uuid "bd399471-0d59-5f1e-81a5-12cf064e3816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249107} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d559c-a304-5add-bf80-843c7bcf1778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd399471-0d59-5f1e-81a5-12cf064e3816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249107} :answer-field/id #uuid "452d4c1f-6d4a-5321-8449-b83136ef15ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c98eb68-5485-54b6-b79b-f365d74db723" :question/math-academy-id "q-115631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,-4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417457e2-f527-590e-8a52-cfbea734e890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248006} {:answer/id #uuid "59f803eb-398e-554f-959f-407e6aa234ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248236} {:answer/id #uuid "772d4b5c-5e0b-5e8c-8d75-4a2c540051b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248474} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0eb5d3-7444-55f0-9242-f2645d848a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248973} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf395d3-b5c3-56fe-82d5-f51343d8cd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "772d4b5c-5e0b-5e8c-8d75-4a2c540051b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248474} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8e70aa-72f7-5f54-9fef-39805521c959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34ce73b1-4d1a-5e32-8244-a4c68ffbd052" :question/math-academy-id "q-115633" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,-2),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ccb079-926a-5d71-a4b5-2a3f7c343416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{8}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247629} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5ba719-5f1e-5166-b9d6-c5226048fe7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247835} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c331-11c0-5df0-bbef-46c82af1a8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247848} {:answer/id #uuid "928e0490-9198-5dea-9500-3e20cad763ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248723} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4e48dc-8a18-50cc-9d72-07b7205dd968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f44c331-11c0-5df0-bbef-46c82af1a8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247848} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9464fef-08cc-5548-b814-d627646cdb2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b141f773-2517-5672-ab61-0399ba3f3a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-115636" :question/problem "![](images/57/5742f3f1161782d06b8a785d28d6c59b1e40aec37bd6b62268d6d41e1c662ee0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(-2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-4,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-2,-1),$ we substitute $h=-2$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-4$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (-4+1)^{2} & =4p(-4+2) \\\\ 9 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{8})(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{9}{8}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} {:answer/id #uuid "839ff22d-c37d-5d91-9333-174668c3261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248587} {:answer/id #uuid "ba27305a-d65b-5604-aaff-169ce339bb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249088} {:answer/id #uuid "c6443236-97c8-5e59-91fe-9a22a7cec2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249186} {:answer/id #uuid "dc878183-0dd0-5e61-ae12-26bab8ba8369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{12}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2674032e-cd4e-5e8a-803c-317914ccae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247762} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f52642-018d-53e4-af4e-c4f058d871f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff1310e-72c2-5e26-9574-a4434ee14fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-115642" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ea1ad85f30978bed681f48e02f4af2f401a1f561390bee09683ead252fc1d7d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(4,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (-3+2)^{2} & =4p(4-1) \\\\ 1 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{12})(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{12}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355fbc00-261f-53ee-8dcf-73fe6e42e797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247905} {:answer/id #uuid "4af1f11c-7e10-50c1-9a5b-ddcd30c9563e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248095} {:answer/id #uuid "58a5fee4-f000-5d2a-a869-da8133138d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248228} {:answer/id #uuid "9e115316-bacf-5821-b13a-ecd916aa4e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248827} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c1e0c0-f378-51f0-a377-0637fecf2b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355fbc00-261f-53ee-8dcf-73fe6e42e797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247905} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cae7d88-3221-5c34-bd20-23e6289bab44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30a0f072-9dc6-5517-92da-25f253588788" :question/math-academy-id "q-115643" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f6330a6e9bc0e48326982c7b7509f3e2257de994c5736c813ede93a07491e70.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,-3),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156c0dd5-6e39-5f04-a2cf-5d5dc02bd4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247617} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbb2f1f-caff-5f66-92f3-069a18a95d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=36(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247854} {:answer/id #uuid "564d352c-519a-586d-9146-7140399b0611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248207} {:answer/id #uuid "724017f7-8d2c-566b-ad71-c51f4c355e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248421} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f76d45-8bcb-51a9-bc6f-283a0972d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=-9(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "724017f7-8d2c-566b-ad71-c51f4c355e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248421} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c24b43-25a5-5bdf-b3ed-324954fa4e5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afe09fb4-eb0a-5d67-bf21-bab7a68c96cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-115646" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-3),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 9 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{8})(x-1) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189c7ea7-e51d-529e-81cd-d08955257660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247645} {:answer/id #uuid "5feb679b-21a6-5f8e-91a9-18154b4df9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{20}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248292} {:answer/id #uuid "6271c6bb-71be-51f3-aa98-5accc41c9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248312} {:answer/id #uuid "8448f536-36df-5394-9b5c-7e6bb99e2ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{20}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248595} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf06e10-c6ad-5a47-8343-5d64e3bc9118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6271c6bb-71be-51f3-aa98-5accc41c9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{5}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248312} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa908d94-7533-5961-8c30-b5936faa3381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "909b1cd7-0685-5536-88b4-2736b32e6e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115648" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,2)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(0,2),$ we substitute $h=0$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (5-2)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 9 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4px \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{20})x \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{5}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34f7450d-9fe8-5185-b9b0-16c4a426024e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247898} {:answer/id #uuid "594fa969-2516-5e10-a314-0167ba360f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248232} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cba1a1-15d5-57d6-a679-1c36e6705554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248894} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ac8149-21da-5253-9b8b-314c31a75b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249199} {:answer/id #uuid "d720af45-ac2f-538e-a42c-0f8e90cb0ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d720af45-ac2f-538e-a42c-0f8e90cb0ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249328} :answer-field/id #uuid "05551464-0f03-5aa1-a716-e066278b117a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6a6caca-6d57-513d-aceb-bcb508f5faff" :question/math-academy-id "q-115649" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,0)$ and passes through the point $(0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,0),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (-2)^{2} & =4p(0-2) \\\\ 4 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423478f8-9aa2-5cda-afb3-17b256545aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} {:answer/id #uuid "4aee625f-94b6-586f-a54a-d0e72230fad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3x+1}-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248094} {:answer/id #uuid "874ccc30-a28e-56ec-9671-fcc5053cb332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{4}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248634} {:answer/id #uuid "98869dd8-a1e3-512a-8c1e-3372b8c5a8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248785} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a83092-cd3f-5932-8afb-77fe5a6631f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{1}{3x+1}+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "423478f8-9aa2-5cda-afb3-17b256545aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248015} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cafb8c1-951c-5e34-ba7e-1900668fa6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "031d8c13-fb73-52e6-afed-79556c97e9a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115656" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{3x+1}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(3x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+1=A(3x+1)^{2}+B(3x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1 & =A{(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)}^{2}+B(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)^{2}+1 & =A(3(0)+1)^{2}+B(3(0)+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(-1)^{2}+1 & =A(3(-1)+1)^{2}+B(3(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 10 & =4A-2B+2 \\\\ 4A-2B & =8 \\\\ 2A-B & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $2A-B=4$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3A & =4-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9{x}^{2}+1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+1}-\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(3x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+\\frac{2}{3(3x+1)}-\\frac{1}{3(3x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54d923c2-b2f8-5d1c-a1fc-68a056f22c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248187} {:answer/id #uuid "990bfcfa-1f99-553c-a8ba-07aa7350fb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248791} {:answer/id #uuid "b521e98f-6c0a-5d30-b8c2-17d7c4a8510a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249044} {:answer/id #uuid "cf02c307-1d50-5567-ac85-0f7d14bc9b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249261} {:answer/id #uuid "dece391e-5beb-53bd-9d1e-fa41c6cd1e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b521e98f-6c0a-5d30-b8c2-17d7c4a8510a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249044} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8347731-1849-5e44-ab0d-b77e59a7eb41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b1b65b0-769f-5dc5-a09c-d74cd95adc54" :question/math-academy-id "q-115665" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+5(0)-2 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+5) \\\\ -2 & =B-5 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x+3}{{x}^{2}+5}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+5=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-2}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d86c2f6-a314-54ab-8dcf-d3faec6a6acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247547} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf83c5f-777a-5606-b25b-ad1a2d27450f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248387} {:answer/id #uuid "884fc272-292f-5726-b2b3-9221568754c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248642} {:answer/id #uuid "bad95a73-618f-506a-a148-60bbb75b007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249092} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d383ca-dc7c-5b9d-8c65-750c36a50197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d383ca-dc7c-5b9d-8c65-750c36a50197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249579} :answer-field/id #uuid "348ab176-46a8-53a5-a44b-b51495ebb88c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fcac2c1-8de9-5c0d-89db-ccf7066e1090" :question/math-academy-id "q-115667" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{D}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $D.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+20 & =(x-2)(x+2)+D({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+20 & =(0-2)(0+2)+D({0}^{2}+8) \\\\ 20 & =-4+8D \\\\ 24 & =8D \\\\ D & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+8=u.$ Then, $x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+20}{(x+2)({x}^{2}+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{4})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf1b20f-3f1e-5d39-b833-924bd37ace5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247541} {:answer/id #uuid "1778ff91-6f16-5705-8283-db705faace3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247636} {:answer/id #uuid "30031e73-eda6-5628-a936-a4c56fda636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247857} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0be9ab-d229-5515-9f4f-676bfd0e8f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249222} {:answer/id #uuid "e64b227d-d62e-52ef-8489-7acf21e9c49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1778ff91-6f16-5705-8283-db705faace3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247636} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f2bda8-8928-56d1-a14f-bbe51de1a1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa383850-ed95-5745-b056-448c57db2176" :question/math-academy-id "q-115673" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}=\\frac{2x+B}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $B.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-27 & =(2x+B)(x+1)-({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-5(0)-27 & =(2(0)+B)(0+1)-({0}^{2}+20) \\\\ -27 & =B-20 \\\\ B & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-7}{{x}^{2}+20}-\\frac{1}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution ${x}^{2}+20=u.$ Then, $2x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u,$ and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+20}\\,\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-27}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+20)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+20)-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}x}{10})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d004b1-8a79-5237-9a43-c5d695947628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247474} {:answer/id #uuid "73d79229-ca1a-597f-bc97-2007a1959580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248441} {:answer/id #uuid "859ad402-4287-5864-9a3a-b27433df0b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248615} {:answer/id #uuid "d816ae40-b699-5487-b9be-edd17a53da31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249333} {:answer/id #uuid "fbddd6e4-1678-53af-9fac-a34da478aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859ad402-4287-5864-9a3a-b27433df0b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248615} :answer-field/id #uuid "39da731f-4eac-5eb0-8967-592e81989b3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54337009-4b4f-594c-8090-7c81fe847987" :question/math-academy-id "q-115686" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-9x+5 & =(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)} & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-9x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c28c1-7366-514f-8a71-70b8574e8b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247456} {:answer/id #uuid "10cab1b6-0eef-57ac-9c3c-e79de55b6805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247573} {:answer/id #uuid "567e1f65-c305-5e5b-8580-4126bb121c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248209} {:answer/id #uuid "aef9856b-bea1-549a-9bcd-ac2c1039a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248991} {:answer/id #uuid "d72b33b4-9d9a-5a6a-bd0d-01f35633bd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72b33b4-9d9a-5a6a-bd0d-01f35633bd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249329} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d57d04-325f-59f4-bb1d-89f0fcd9bb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "412d22df-d958-56dd-8884-22116d9638ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-115690" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+6$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+6}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+6)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x & =(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-4,$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)} & =\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}+6}-\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+6| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}}).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-{x}^{2}-7x}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6)-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{6}})-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06faf40-267a-585a-a698-8d1c4a8418b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248857} {:answer/id #uuid "b11b9a13-576c-58f2-b1fa-068e66f1591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249010} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c1daa1-28ba-5945-bf62-2d01df1e7ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249162} {:answer/id #uuid "e31429d4-0443-599a-a3a4-365a3f4492c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249430} {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7c76-3253-5217-8050-0e4ee5b66e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebec7c76-3253-5217-8050-0e4ee5b66e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249496} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adf0cfe-17c4-56d0-b154-4a4709b2b3f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b36c8e07-4439-57ae-b9d1-276ef485c6ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-115693" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+3$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+3)(x+4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+5x-9 & =(Ax+B)(x+4)+C({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)} & =\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+3| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+5x-9}{({x}^{2}+3)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+3)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}x}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e0f5cbb-98e8-55aa-b890-984b305ed4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248530} {:answer/id #uuid "94947933-b100-53c7-8fdb-0b6d8803da1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248745} {:answer/id #uuid "95219ef7-6026-599f-b799-3fe1f3c2a17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248749} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d1e02-75e2-51b5-87f3-47ea9e6004dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249502} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a858d-c201-59de-aa39-33d3a1e7d443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec9d1e02-75e2-51b5-87f3-47ea9e6004dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249502} :answer-field/id #uuid "d433bef3-6036-5aac-9df3-3dc1353dab2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74feace7-d730-58bc-b03e-4652e0e5c170" :question/math-academy-id "q-115710" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(4)^{2}-4(1)(-6)=40\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{40}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+4x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+12x-12=A({x}^{2}+4x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=2$, and $C=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+4x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+4)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}+12x-12}{x({x}^{2}+4x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{2}+4x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+4x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ddb4ed-90f0-5df5-b21a-41d53d8fe661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}{3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248021} {:answer/id #uuid "49d41c54-3f94-52cd-a66b-4ba669efe7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248079} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f4069d-2ea6-59d1-acc4-c6d742ba2c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d86ae-66d8-5482-ac29-aa13eed6597e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249254} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c6e3ba-2427-5970-9188-62ee0b23275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1c6e3ba-2427-5970-9188-62ee0b23275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249284} :answer-field/id #uuid "6905e022-77c6-597f-b4cb-140b5c642224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d0e590b-4ba3-5c1f-9a3a-31d9c817ed33" :question/math-academy-id "q-115712" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  $\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(5)^{2}-4(2)(-6)=73\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{73}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6),$ and we get\n\n$$\n12-5x=A(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)+(Bx+C)x\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=4$, and $C=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}=-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}+5x-6.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12-5x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x-6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4x+5}{2{x}^{2}+5x-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}+5x-6|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2e5bd3-f473-510b-9434-aa7443cb05b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247670} {:answer/id #uuid "1d717368-1df9-5a63-8977-b45159997003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247687} {:answer/id #uuid "89616672-693d-59fb-88ad-7b1432546374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248644} {:answer/id #uuid "a9969d54-7454-5477-b1ca-0b4165edbcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248931} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ccf359-d194-5c0f-99a1-734608e48ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d717368-1df9-5a63-8977-b45159997003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247687} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d962fb6-9b43-5d0e-8fc2-499dbcc92713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "734835be-6e59-529d-977d-cd321f0badd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115772" :question/problem "Given that $r=\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=7$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot (1-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27edf7e2-1949-5b19-8951-ef3c63f01da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247781} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea79f41-fe25-516b-bca1-bc3026a8967b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247839} {:answer/id #uuid "8417dd2f-c817-521f-8b3a-8a613dc15c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248592} {:answer/id #uuid "a49939f7-f7ec-5933-b7fb-eda0850f82be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248891} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd0cdbd-93c2-547d-a725-3c921538ee20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8417dd2f-c817-521f-8b3a-8a613dc15c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248592} :answer-field/id #uuid "df247728-6fc4-5d6e-ad05-31984fda9cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b468347a-5af4-5e0d-add1-8cb76b34a17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115776" :question/problem "Given that $r=-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 24\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (24\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{16} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aacad592-c837-5a68-8f8b-57c3d7dcb43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248943} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d09c12-1f86-5e78-8465-e8c7d48b9e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249017} {:answer/id #uuid "b2637471-8d8a-5bbe-bd12-5b87dfc106b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249023} {:answer/id #uuid "d0726f1e-da5c-51b1-9a97-44846fb7928e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249271} {:answer/id #uuid "f632b040-af98-517b-9654-3f33648e9c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2637471-8d8a-5bbe-bd12-5b87dfc106b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249023} :answer-field/id #uuid "02e92776-40a0-56fe-8e7e-05efd3ee58e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b056bc9-1b86-5285-9289-f5246eedec51" :question/math-academy-id "q-115788" :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}=\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $m=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by ${m}^{3},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+\\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}}) & ={m}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}}) \\\\ {m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{{m}^{3}}+{m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{{m}^{3}} & ={m}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{{m}^{3}} \\\\ \\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{3m-1}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}}+\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} & =\\cancel{{m}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+3}{\\cancel{{m}^{3}}} \\\\ 3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3m-1+m+2 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m+1 & =2m+3 \\\\ 4m-2m & =3-1 \\\\ 2m & =2 \\\\ m & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=1\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b0fb4a6-a0a8-5f73-8a19-abd9ab8d7b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247947} {:answer/id #uuid "512c886f-c605-5743-8740-b7c0b7952acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248153} {:answer/id #uuid "a792d170-bee0-5203-810d-3149d20c6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248914} {:answer/id #uuid "b68ef072-78e4-53b5-8670-2baeaf867b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249060} {:answer/id #uuid "b69e7dcd-1896-5e5e-b67d-4d01a47dee7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a792d170-bee0-5203-810d-3149d20c6d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248914} :answer-field/id #uuid "87bed9b6-d71c-5c36-af6d-4aec4c0b6168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb3909a1-5d95-5bcb-832e-e8e09e292f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-115791" :question/problem "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z+1}{2z}=2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $z=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ 2z\\cdot (\\frac{z+1}{2z}) & =2z\\cdot (2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}) \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2z\\cdot 2-2z\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ \\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} & =2z\\cdot 2-\\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ z+1 & =4z-(2z+1) \\\\ z+1 & =4z-2z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =4z-2z-1 \\\\ z+1 & =2z-1 \\\\ -z & =-2 \\\\ z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db9a03b-7189-51c6-beb4-58184628399a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247551} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffff95f-e0da-51b7-b2b1-60ad819c5e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247856} {:answer/id #uuid "5a100c5a-10c6-507e-ab3d-717c80187730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248239} {:answer/id #uuid "de207cc8-0ebf-5357-9d93-bba635b2e97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249392} {:answer/id #uuid "fc55d1bf-5b4e-5900-8dba-dfc04d82fcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc55d1bf-5b4e-5900-8dba-dfc04d82fcc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249625} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c87277-896d-50dd-82b5-385c9dad90a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c001b720-cfa8-54e2-8c25-eb8cf8283524" :question/math-academy-id "q-115792" :question/problem "Solve for $w$ where $1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}=\\frac{w+5}{3w}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $w=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $3w,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =\\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w\\cdot (1-\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =3w\\cdot (\\frac{w+5}{3w}) \\\\ 3w\\cdot 1-3w\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =3w\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{3w} \\\\ 3w-\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{2w-3}{\\cancel{3w}} & =\\cancel{3w}\\cdot \\frac{w+5}{\\cancel{3w}} \\\\ 3w-(2w-3) & =w+5 \\\\ 3w-2w+3 & =w+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-2w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w+3 & =w+5 \\\\ w-w & =5-3 \\\\ 0 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} {:answer/id #uuid "284a2d2d-aec2-56b2-a83a-447fe88b345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247782} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd2d177-bb80-5fbb-b4fb-239d54aa4023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248101} {:answer/id #uuid "9a88c5c8-97bc-5272-bbaa-7a0f74513dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248802} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeb1cf3-0a3b-5e0b-81f5-f88d8973a54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bbe6c94-b151-5c1d-9c24-6ac6eaedff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247527} :answer-field/id #uuid "929d68dd-7d98-50ea-b0e6-bb084af9ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a05ffdc-3b52-5cee-9869-369fd44c61d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115794" :question/problem "Which value of $z$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $z=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3z$ and $6z.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3z,6z)=6z.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z-1}{6z}=\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z} \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z}+\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ 6z\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{6z}) & =6z\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{3z})+6z\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{6z}) \\\\ \\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{2z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3z}\\cdot (\\frac{3z-1}{\\cancel{3z}})+\\cancel{6z}\\cdot (\\frac{z-1}{\\cancel{6z}}) \\\\ 2z-1 & =2\\cdot (3z-1)+z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-1 & =2(3z-1)+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =6z-2+z-1 \\\\ 2z-1 & =7z-3 \\\\ 2z-7z & =-3+1 \\\\ -5z & =-2 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096c553b-7828-57f6-b436-0ebba6db180e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247506} {:answer/id #uuid "3662123b-4884-519f-a2c9-aa488f53fa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247914} {:answer/id #uuid "92fdc3b4-daad-5ee8-aaeb-bc28ccc8412a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248727} {:answer/id #uuid "9af98b09-2493-587d-aaf7-02ccdebddcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248804} {:answer/id #uuid "d40225e2-a3b2-5686-908b-b61ab91cd370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af98b09-2493-587d-aaf7-02ccdebddcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248804} :answer-field/id #uuid "144839cb-882f-5a1a-a1fa-8d94766d1746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a80168e-4de0-55ab-b42f-115076b5300e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115898" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=6y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{3}{2})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65fe4c0d-ace7-531e-beb7-ee58e3de3451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248342} {:answer/id #uuid "74587b67-e4e7-5697-a5a6-6030edcf2fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248449} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f72a67-c47a-5be9-ad72-37a4d337a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248933} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc96723-add8-5dc4-9b11-f81c31894208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249379} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc93ce4-82f8-54be-85e4-eb320b4e4a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc93ce4-82f8-54be-85e4-eb320b4e4a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249634} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b1ce8f9-82c3-51dc-9505-eea5dfb49165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b53a4d3-8dde-5b34-8af3-3a4b04e9f45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115905" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(\\frac{1}{2})x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106bf08a-6288-5161-b589-b3a6bfd5d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247568} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecf6f9d-a2f5-586a-b320-3685a62fe9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248538} {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} {:answer/id #uuid "e75adbb9-dff5-55d0-b063-3054aec88c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249463} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0d9b69-2f3f-545f-8357-505ac2a681cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d436ef0-528d-5c42-acfd-fecb45ade3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248822} :answer-field/id #uuid "791f4c31-853e-56a1-9a6c-8d9759f6db39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ec04397-a1d2-5a43-be90-9c3301fd7dc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115911" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-1)^{2}=5(x+7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+7).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-7$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-7,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0918bb01-088a-5fb8-9976-6affbd0c602e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247502} {:answer/id #uuid "54e90a0d-89ad-5674-86ec-1963b16305e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248189} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6bd378-5100-5d78-bfdc-40d8310a4f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248286} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a03cc9-862b-55ef-af02-5e36a687bb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248869} {:answer/id #uuid "d19f41ed-1b0f-5895-92ef-f2629810f757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6bd378-5100-5d78-bfdc-40d8310a4f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248286} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c21528-e209-5727-b3ee-515c255eaaf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27f3a217-c646-5edd-93b0-a8b079b9f019" :question/math-academy-id "q-115919" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three-fifths of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0237b559-7d33-5cd0-9da8-8d8b205f4742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247438} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb99e31-a544-5220-80a8-c1892b899c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247705} {:answer/id #uuid "cac77d12-ae34-5ddd-8afb-806b1d42068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249230} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} {:answer/id #uuid "fc368912-431d-5c1c-94c8-d1e252061d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2d99394-c6b7-5118-9b32-40e3e577b39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249294} :answer-field/id #uuid "05de44ca-2588-5951-97b7-06f8b0b05597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f403d57-f6d2-5901-9ad6-4def1edb7127" :question/math-academy-id "q-116104" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and ${x}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,{x}^{2})={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${x}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{{x}^{2}}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x})-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3x-1}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) & =x\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\cancel{x}})-\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{x-3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\ (3x-1) & =x\\cdot 2-(x-3) \\\\ (3x-1) & =2x-(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1) & =2x-(x-3) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x-x+3 \\\\ 3x-1 & =x+3 \\\\ 3x-x & =3+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095b621d-d28c-5438-ac86-bb537204c4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247505} {:answer/id #uuid "19938bf6-8fa9-534a-99f3-a34885b9ec72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247656} {:answer/id #uuid "4194167a-a248-589b-8287-fdb853c46d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248008} {:answer/id #uuid "f3500a3c-8d1a-531b-a225-4a4c4972bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249554} {:answer/id #uuid "f647ed95-e4d5-5ab0-baa1-dad4fc8d3414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3500a3c-8d1a-531b-a225-4a4c4972bd3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249554} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bf22087-6398-52e3-9c0b-1effe99dbc91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3783baa6-61fe-5e5f-a6e5-bd47f3137af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-116106" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2{m}^{2},$ ${m}^{2}$ and $m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,{m}^{2},2{m}^{2})=2{m}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{m}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m})-2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}})-\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{m}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot 3-(3m+4) & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6m-(3m+4) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6m-(3m+4) & =2 \\\\ 6m-3m-4 & =2 \\\\ 3m & =6 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7b94ae-5c5d-5a90-9f9b-48dff29de3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250659} {:answer/id #uuid "4a18df07-f7d9-59d3-8f70-e9b3b6738591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251169} {:answer/id #uuid "8e36fe03-f4cf-58d9-97e7-5ee23757234d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251756} {:answer/id #uuid "91f5f78b-03ee-59f5-849c-c8de47775dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251801} {:answer/id #uuid "f469076d-5ab7-5e45-85bf-58ca2c786c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a18df07-f7d9-59d3-8f70-e9b3b6738591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251169} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24ab2b9-6b03-53c1-a30b-f4718e0e1f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10207108-a210-580a-9bc1-6306b708682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-116650" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4fa141d5af6f23e2eaa1d416def8f84e21e48f775b5b195cbc8ed74e4bb64218.png)\n\nA circular iceberg is melting in such a way that the superficial circle is decreasing its area at a rate of $24\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\text{/year}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the iceberg is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $6\\,\\text{km}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=6,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=6\\,\\text{km},$ $C=2\\pi (6)=12\\pi \\text{ km}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi $ and $C=12\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{12\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{24\\pi }{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ km/year}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059ba794-5e1e-5457-bdea-b64dbf8af255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250598} {:answer/id #uuid "10b69a39-7e8c-5f74-896a-d443bd7304be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250697} {:answer/id #uuid "11354cf1-977d-5955-8b92-e5f5eb109c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250702} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b5fbcf-da28-5414-abe5-919e7481946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252162} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e20126-30f4-5509-99b2-6d09012d8ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b69a39-7e8c-5f74-896a-d443bd7304be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250697} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f0404d-47dd-51ab-b2d1-37abcadbb9fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d27a8b9-840b-5e81-b68e-56b475bcaf90" :question/math-academy-id "q-116798" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-5)+4,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-5)+4 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (0-5)+4 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{3})≈2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca01609-0a8b-5ee1-a86e-9ea04ed6fe2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251741} {:answer/id #uuid "a8615bcd-5800-5dd9-ba76-7facffd6dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252010} {:answer/id #uuid "b96a0caa-65dd-5577-be12-ca3294e512b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252184} {:answer/id #uuid "c40f88e6-74ad-5084-bfce-8992e7981c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252291} {:answer/id #uuid "de30bdd3-59c6-5f53-9fd6-3692956c6922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8615bcd-5800-5dd9-ba76-7facffd6dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252010} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb629470-c03e-5a0f-8d5d-fca92c2a3682" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2cb32bd7-bce1-5088-9a7e-4c63d6e35647" :question/math-academy-id "q-116800" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)={e}^{2}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{2}-2=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | ${e}^{2}+2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{5}{2})≈{e}^{2}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dfb223-94da-59c9-93d7-40747b254902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250895} {:answer/id #uuid "632efb00-5e1f-5eeb-87a6-be90c76ce2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251364} {:answer/id #uuid "965b4e6d-0b10-5173-a0ba-bc2dedcc6a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251843} {:answer/id #uuid "9d818da9-724c-5b6d-b0fc-dc9670a79bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251914} {:answer/id #uuid "f084042b-4913-5aa5-b6ed-ff51f6655714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27dfb223-94da-59c9-93d7-40747b254902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250895} :answer-field/id #uuid "a390b7c9-522f-5c15-b279-5cf16154b3ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1718b71d-74bc-5780-811c-5db2e873c658" :question/math-academy-id "q-116886" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(-1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+(-3) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10fd5935-a6d2-5b39-acf7-bb6003859673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250700} {:answer/id #uuid "63f04560-efb0-5870-a4e2-32593e791830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251371} {:answer/id #uuid "de5ef8ec-701c-5c7c-b90e-4ebe987fc175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252511} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7886ed-0b78-565a-a9c1-1ea94ef119b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252641} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1e21c1-72e7-550f-b287-010bb2b39415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f04560-efb0-5870-a4e2-32593e791830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251371} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af55769-4924-594b-ba0e-ed85228b1c66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd07643b-ca8f-5e00-9ad5-265bd5d31225" :question/math-academy-id "q-117408" :question/problem "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62398511-9191-54bf-9678-afe8b77b6df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} {:answer/id #uuid "76e51c07-ae30-5f76-9211-c622eb4edaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251534} {:answer/id #uuid "7db2cbba-40a7-517d-85d4-4db5c32721c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251608} {:answer/id #uuid "c42fdbb0-808e-537e-ae57-9e0a4f2d9b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252292} {:answer/id #uuid "e30522a4-eaa9-513c-932a-1eb516b48504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62398511-9191-54bf-9678-afe8b77b6df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251351} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e46c9e-0f1c-54e1-8afd-4cf347c19516" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a5d4f00-fa86-576c-9cad-eafafdb18fe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-117411" :question/problem "$-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4506a2a0-4a66-51aa-a003-d9f643a2f7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251125} {:answer/id #uuid "646e1641-365e-53ec-bf58-e219647ee54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251375} {:answer/id #uuid "6897262f-9c63-5884-a06a-50434981336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251411} {:answer/id #uuid "93a9ff23-0972-5e3c-9993-18fea9dd977c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251821} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d322b5-3e3c-5f03-9243-09ddd91e5e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d322b5-3e3c-5f03-9243-09ddd91e5e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252186} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a35cff-79b6-5d48-8d7a-ed6977457c32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fdb4740-bcc2-5647-bec0-b3bf354a5523" :question/math-academy-id "q-117412" :question/problem "$10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e104022-bb4e-565d-a6bf-bd962d901ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251070} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7c7f6e-d175-5ced-b466-3c36c4f17838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ea3bf9-1407-5df8-8f30-0caede0decbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252322} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e6f228-8f8a-57cb-9af2-282ce558e2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252406} {:answer/id #uuid "eef4021b-6b37-5e54-b681-f27585eee147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7c7f6e-d175-5ced-b466-3c36c4f17838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251317} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48b917e-69fa-56ff-b770-34926780baa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b40d2d1-a796-5057-96c3-9515dd6af44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117442" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3{x}^{3}+24$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+24 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{3} & =-24 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-3,-1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n![](images/80/80315b4862adf8ebb1f91f5c4c7606a59dfa5738c40e34179a745e03a0a25693.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-3,-2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot ([4-16]-[\\frac{81}{4}-24]) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-12+\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{33}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{99}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{x}^{3}+24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+8x)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot ([\\frac{1}{4}-8]-[4-16]) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{4}+12) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{99}{4}+\\frac{51}{4}=\\frac{75}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57317c5d-547c-5676-9794-216eb7820aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251266} {:answer/id #uuid "5e49eea4-11c0-55ab-a94f-bf0ce604cfa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251331} {:answer/id #uuid "629a7e52-8f4e-5bed-bb8c-c8b4074dbe61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251355} {:answer/id #uuid "b9119585-69d4-5571-a90b-11589d386ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252182} {:answer/id #uuid "c66a43b9-c276-5c36-80e8-c1ebb31d2aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9119585-69d4-5571-a90b-11589d386ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252182} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b32753c-b724-5f6f-aa1e-07ed9f98d5ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9f3c4b9-a238-51ae-b5c9-d1395c8e83c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117457" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85660ae9-4f2e-5621-a014-ab47c7110fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251671} {:answer/id #uuid "862e8743-0a18-5e52-9f70-2ff15d02a428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251678} {:answer/id #uuid "ae14b8c7-3fd3-53d4-beb7-4b473c691e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252070} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c548d1-7499-5fd9-b385-aaaac7b68c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252098} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04858f-5250-51d1-ade7-ddf22507a99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd04858f-5250-51d1-ade7-ddf22507a99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252361} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8709c71-c949-55ae-bbde-e386757bf140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ae088f2-83ab-519c-8adf-de49960f46a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-117459" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =3\\cdot ({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091dfe6d-dbcd-5dc8-908f-031833bf5679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250636} {:answer/id #uuid "37d5087b-6859-5f3e-8798-9a0961309a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251014} {:answer/id #uuid "690726ad-749f-5890-8274-26648cc3d209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251420} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e69f0d-76ca-5918-8497-cea75026a9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252108} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c9e48f-2c4b-51db-b2d5-abde59af8121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d5087b-6859-5f3e-8798-9a0961309a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251014} :answer-field/id #uuid "938dd839-cb93-5473-921d-1230977f6699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "faf88325-d673-5e64-a0a2-e5898606cfcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-117461" :question/problem "$-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) & =(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ecc819-acb1-5df5-9748-8605bfca9317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251015} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0f002-cae4-5c37-8a69-4b724f88ce62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251413} {:answer/id #uuid "81c81ed5-e80c-5c65-a8b3-082014a382f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251648} {:answer/id #uuid "990be92e-936a-5c07-9036-ee48d86256d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251868} {:answer/id #uuid "ce85ac4a-bc9b-5c3a-8299-d9d1ea40fe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68a0f002-cae4-5c37-8a69-4b724f88ce62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251413} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac16ee52-49d1-5b8b-9170-ffd9df8262f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24e984a2-585a-5608-b3a2-5f94b69b88d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-117462" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05263274-8a30-5b9e-99c8-bc4632800973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{2},6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250593} {:answer/id #uuid "26c93fc6-e7c9-527e-b808-8e8ae2deae84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250886} {:answer/id #uuid "622a263a-6ba2-5b98-88d5-099953d3f597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251350} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5a5357-7eb2-5b78-bbad-edc691df5fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251758} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9086da-236a-59d4-9d03-b14bbfe51a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd9086da-236a-59d4-9d03-b14bbfe51a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252777} :answer-field/id #uuid "49dfe913-70a3-528c-b8ca-29e7f976f6c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8647f1c-5367-5a91-bc69-e78441ce9412" :question/math-academy-id "q-117463" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79b4f2fe-61fe-595e-9afc-66fee93d0af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251565} {:answer/id #uuid "99d6d289-0be0-5b5b-a332-7709c8440710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251875} {:answer/id #uuid "a110bdeb-4b4c-5c86-a664-7f5138d2c1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251943} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5205e0-26dd-5e9a-ba86-f903d4f49d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252050} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1632aa-3461-5d5c-a789-47a490ec4b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a110bdeb-4b4c-5c86-a664-7f5138d2c1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251943} :answer-field/id #uuid "01ac0480-1bc1-5114-8725-a56fa87f797d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52486dc2-a5ad-5323-887e-ec059a62d92e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11753" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133fede6-e6b3-5934-9ab6-600b897e76c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb9ca10-8ea3-5cda-814e-af155fd838bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c06d53-55cc-51d9-bc54-73807258d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252328} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c2b286-9473-5923-a0df-50419f5281e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252465} {:answer/id #uuid "ef673879-992e-531a-9cc6-362a14d4f889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb9ca10-8ea3-5cda-814e-af155fd838bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1d9cb5-e489-5d90-befc-482609c96a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b52b18c-f18d-51d2-9931-b8f3ebe75944" :question/math-academy-id "q-11756" :question/problem "![](images/58/58c92cc542f027ec882191d2b4631f9fcf3704820b2825a18f30dbdc3f59d07b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-4,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,2),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a66e0c-95d3-55b9-b3ba-7a5a5e78709a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250992} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd4c776-c0be-5732-a407-f7edbca1a409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251066} {:answer/id #uuid "57e53e6d-b266-517e-ad73-38153bdd8c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251270} {:answer/id #uuid "aabb06ee-3ceb-5738-9f76-0e5f376bcff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252032} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb24956-8a43-55e6-a408-c64426c8f93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aabb06ee-3ceb-5738-9f76-0e5f376bcff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252032} :answer-field/id #uuid "45857773-a925-5062-b5dc-38ca097e9df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ad97703-d400-5d43-94f9-d8eae5764f59" :question/math-academy-id "q-11757" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4161544d-5503-509c-a919-47b75546d693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251094} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0b8f2d-8bf9-5548-96dd-fe899f5d298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252036} {:answer/id #uuid "ba23df8f-a679-5d5c-bde8-2b7238d24224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252191} {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} {:answer/id #uuid "f25fe0b5-3a2d-5c1e-b5a3-425fbf7a30c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} :answer-field/id #uuid "918ffa31-2155-56a2-9928-59d9d880148e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53685d44-d788-5019-bc63-97c441bd2453" :question/math-academy-id "q-117578" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71fbeca3-4760-5d6a-856c-a573ea317eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{300}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251486} {:answer/id #uuid "9229fa93-9ffb-5473-9b2e-ff4e454a6679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251803} {:answer/id #uuid "ca888844-ac33-5b63-a641-8bce7947f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{480}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252347} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fdabb1-db44-594d-8452-62e77cc9289f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252685} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9b6765-ab75-54b3-a097-f8617bfd508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9229fa93-9ffb-5473-9b2e-ff4e454a6679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251803} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf475618-9da6-5ade-89c7-309940393d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0d5cec2-04d5-542c-903e-28cdf3f5b313" :question/math-academy-id "q-117579" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3}-{45}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}={270}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e99e15-33a7-50a8-b257-d2f54fa47ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250852} {:answer/id #uuid "3d59c05e-c0fa-5cfc-9ca6-d8e6cdd02580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251062} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2d0726-54ec-57ea-a54a-c02384eca19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251735} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8b0abc-64b9-5797-bc2a-b55cdc58fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251903} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ee460a-830e-5d43-910b-fb39d4b81f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2d0726-54ec-57ea-a54a-c02384eca19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15068e8-bb8c-5630-a436-266e3b67d205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42337ba6-a7cb-5434-9483-5084bb012a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11762" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a2f289efe5b8f44c27b5d1ffe6d46d58a658f7f81155736288bc621909dd7727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,6),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 36 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29405a5d-23a6-502b-be5d-56f1c497a84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250905} {:answer/id #uuid "718e38f3-a5c7-5905-95a6-c54405c63cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251484} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4bf91-0eba-544f-95ca-a4cafe2e4918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251514} {:answer/id #uuid "a800d469-ec72-5341-b219-06608ed25eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252001} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6e430e-c227-57e4-9f29-28cf38e29ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29405a5d-23a6-502b-be5d-56f1c497a84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250905} :answer-field/id #uuid "13384956-5f4c-5ced-a1de-1fad438fa14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58d5c577-c726-5c8e-a1f0-5cd965b1b3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117637" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$, the $y$-axis, and lines  $y=1$ and $y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{y}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-e)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8eea0e-4cb9-5909-8a9c-73b546a569cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250812} {:answer/id #uuid "50846ea1-2a20-5022-b2c6-92023bf743ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251211} {:answer/id #uuid "c12cfbc4-0a87-57a5-b8bc-f3e3efb188bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252256} {:answer/id #uuid "d71160f7-9b73-5cde-ae0c-a48912324d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252461} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6802c-c089-5ea3-b944-d80f3ffb063f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6802c-c089-5ea3-b944-d80f3ffb063f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252689} :answer-field/id #uuid "95be207c-6257-5213-aca8-fa8e283f89d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "547a5328-8c82-5389-8e2c-39909fcd12b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-11764" :question/problem "![](images/83/83439b98fff25c021a2713448779167f25a0b9f04dd0d14b115a55f915e3501d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(12,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(12,6),$ we can substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b11be4-3658-52c8-8bf0-25fdceb40fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3ea915-9740-5a98-a33d-698d64c00a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251179} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4e7811-a45b-5e5c-98c0-b14ff8d1be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251199} {:answer/id #uuid "9d77d381-d721-506e-9675-3e44eb48d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251912} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb9a8c-18f6-586a-9cc9-426dc5df378c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49b11be4-3658-52c8-8bf0-25fdceb40fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251165} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ed92ee-7925-517a-a895-7f73c4059a69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50009c8b-7a58-5669-927f-169a6eff91b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117658" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3})+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3})+4=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=3,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c3c9aa-fed0-5631-b8da-f009c3d807d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251012} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbd6e53-ceba-56c7-abe2-a9d6ba652a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251206} {:answer/id #uuid "938c0634-0cbb-5e7e-b7d4-a95b9ff51bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251819} {:answer/id #uuid "a18d3f2c-bc11-502d-bf5d-b6b990acac82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251945} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fb0798-47ee-5cc7-9714-d66de9e94630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fbd6e53-ceba-56c7-abe2-a9d6ba652a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251206} :answer-field/id #uuid "bed4769b-f371-55d8-9550-13873ddaaded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a5543e1-6bbc-55cb-a040-aa25b0325f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11766" :question/problem "![](images/67/6723e1c76d63216151e7c78d67c882ae98f9af950675e37ced726e8ce5aea4d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-12,8),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2430686f-6354-5ae3-a56a-86797016a196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250867} {:answer/id #uuid "84c30401-b464-5d98-8a9b-267f533cca26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251667} {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} {:answer/id #uuid "a5606c09-671a-5dc4-abd2-15172f36984e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251979} {:answer/id #uuid "ab67f75e-df9d-5d97-89f6-287ab3d2483f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5b3662-7c4a-554a-a3fa-0abb3a6d9754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc8f3fd2-a7ad-59ae-81ee-947a33e17f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-117661" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4})=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=2,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1748716a-02da-5dc5-ad1d-869916cb8e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250758} {:answer/id #uuid "4658d1c7-fd54-59be-b2ad-96c992324dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251136} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5b2a35-69b6-5924-b604-4fc93e71c429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251194} {:answer/id #uuid "56e08bbc-f4bf-5a8e-83a7-7758acfe336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251265} {:answer/id #uuid "d092f162-e55c-5327-bd24-d88c4db777ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d5b2a35-69b6-5924-b604-4fc93e71c429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251194} :answer-field/id #uuid "00c73b43-39e5-5c62-8c73-77a6438f3b09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-fie