:answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250568} {:answer/id #uuid "36b69884-e0f8-5a35-a86f-6504c958fffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251003} {:answer/id #uuid "6c61d383-b5d6-55e5-89aa-69e7219ae6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251442} {:answer/id #uuid "9c25fc1b-e751-5f4d-a4ea-2c79ebdd5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251901} {:answer/id #uuid "b600ef2d-7430-52c3-9a6b-33649cc4dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b600ef2d-7430-52c3-9a6b-33649cc4dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252153} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c419e4-fed3-5638-9bd2-e528284bfe38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4844153a-cf84-57ba-8cb5-907264c75fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-11779" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b09f63373bba1bb796f1832a4c7aa33ec220186e75bb84ccc79dcc46b662636e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-12).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-12),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136e9a50-a407-5dae-bb44-6830ed03634d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250721} {:answer/id #uuid "71247e13-a93b-5580-b517-d8ff9b498733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251477} {:answer/id #uuid "878a709f-3d04-5bfc-a930-c231fd8f4d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251695} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd379a2-c52a-5f4e-8e55-bf89541c660a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252209} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2a82e3-36c7-5494-9ee6-1f44ba1cd0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "136e9a50-a407-5dae-bb44-6830ed03634d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250721} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf25f51e-e56f-50b4-a8b7-6e2bb7244cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f5a48a1-5998-564e-8067-a60e891e1877" :question/math-academy-id "q-117795" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f920d90aa215300e720ace10bfec9292440d20f32c9b907854b984014f77427.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec68e9c-38e6-559a-a887-a8091041ac37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250683} {:answer/id #uuid "4d694e8b-7771-57ad-9c4a-86d77c9ded0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251195} {:answer/id #uuid "58dfdc7e-2c25-5c4d-b4d1-2a6e2b08606c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251283} {:answer/id #uuid "afb9e314-7855-5834-b616-8d65e3144e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252086} {:answer/id #uuid "fc68c657-d12f-59d8-bfa1-b90e0b393d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58dfdc7e-2c25-5c4d-b4d1-2a6e2b08606c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251283} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6f51877-7815-5bb3-9f0e-484d066099ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0fa25dc-6bee-5930-931e-86bd1acea862" :question/math-academy-id "q-117798" :question/problem "![](images/45/454c2746d17f6444a9fa5762baae3927fb72eff6a7e51a1df38bc4f325a4a830.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ units up. Hence, $D=2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+2=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235fd882-5110-59ee-a1e5-8d11855075da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250856} {:answer/id #uuid "7622bb9f-c5f3-5f32-969e-e995445640b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251524} {:answer/id #uuid "8c81d441-7f34-5323-9426-fda6851b2d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251739} {:answer/id #uuid "d578743c-119d-54fb-8e3f-7d59d8673902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252445} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fbad45-0af4-5538-9c2d-b46d7e2b12d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d578743c-119d-54fb-8e3f-7d59d8673902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252445} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebfa8d92-cd88-5898-b22c-46f756efc46d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15fef2c4-3c6f-5cf8-99dc-a49b56e65469" :question/math-academy-id "q-117800" :question/problem "![](images/60/6003a85e0038b7e6562381cd338a7597877d1152adbeb476b7141eee74ba06ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d74283e-5ccb-5927-b7c2-7b6dc7348729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250940} {:answer/id #uuid "49d88741-afb9-5f1a-89bc-fa72f184e234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251167} {:answer/id #uuid "53cc6df8-4837-502d-9577-0769a855428d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251238} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e45b1d-95f4-5298-b7f6-badd3269cd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252418} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf0ab8-8236-5a51-9644-727fd4c758d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf0ab8-8236-5a51-9644-727fd4c758d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252495} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9081df-efa4-55bb-b2fa-1ac432b3fbd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73e0be4b-b8c0-5d2c-baec-36cb996c3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-11781" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e588a9250583251ba2fd85b789eb3a856970fb6e7044289257a4ed97e3bbf1a0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
